  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 338 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nFor today, vertically stacked low pressure currently over \ncentral Manitoba/far western Ontario will continue sliding \neastward. An associated cold frontal boundary will slide through\nthe forecast area today switching winds to the northwest and \naiding in a cooling trend that will continue through Sunday \nnight. Similar to yesterday, a tightening pressure gradient from\nthis low will produce windy conditions, with the strongest \nwinds in the southwest. Also similarly to yesterday, a lack of \nstrong CAA and pressure rises should generally keep winds \nsub-advisory level. This aligns with the EFI, which remains \nunimpressed for even advisory level winds. Still, the momentum \ntransfer tool in BUFKIT continues to mix down stronger wind \ngusts at times. Therefore, can't completely rule out a few gusts\nthat reach 45 mph, but believe winds will remain sub-advisory \nfor most locations, most of the day. \n\nShortwave energy off aforementioned low will likely produce a\nmix of light rain and snow showers today and into tonight that \nshould appear streaky on radar. It's hard to say where these \nsnow showers will set-up exactly, so the probability for rain or\nsnow at any particular location at any given time remains low. \nFurthermore, little to to snow accumulation is expected for \nanywhere that does in fact see snow showers. Nevertheless, with \ngusty winds this afternoon and early evening, reductions in \nvisibility are possible during any organized snow showers that \ndo develop.\n\nFor tonight through Sunday, surface low will lift northeastward\ntowards Hudson Bay while Canadian high pressure filters into \nthe state Sunday. As such, winds will become light and often\nvariable with near average temperatures Sunday and Sunday night.\nFor next week, models are in good agreement that another ridge\nwill develop over the Rockies and gradually slide eastward. This\nwill result in another round of rising temperatures that should\nreach their crescendo somewhere around mid-week before ensembles\nfavor another cooling trend for the latter half of the week.\nAn overall split flow pattern is progged to continue to prevail \nfor the foreseeable future. So while an occasional passing \nshortwave could always produce a light precipitation event here \nand there, highly impactful and widespread winter weather \nappears unlikely through at least the next workweek.",
